  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Divers found a half-mile line of stone blocks underwater. September 1968. Eighteen feet down, off North Bimini in the Bahamas.",
        "visual_query": "bahamas shallow turquoise water",
        "visual_fallback": "turquoise shallows"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Roughly rectangular blocks, two to four metres across, corners worn perfectly round. Laid out in one straight line, like giant loaves of bread.",
        "visual_query": "underwater limestone rock formation",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater rocks"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The claims came fast. A lost civilisation. Atlantis. Even a story that a Chinese admiral built it in 1421. So who cut these stones?",
        "visual_query": "caribbean island aerial reef",
        "visual_fallback": "island reef"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nobody. It is beachrock \u2014 shell and sand cemented into limestone, native to the Bahamas, which cracks along straight natural joints as it breaks up.",
        "visual_query": "coastal limestone beachrock shore",
        "visual_fallback": "limestone shore"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Radiocarbon puts the shells near 3,500 years old, and the sea uncovered them roughly 2,000 years ago. The same pavements form off Tasmania and in Utah.",
        "visual_query": "tessellated rock pavement coast",
        "visual_fallback": "rock pavement"
      }
    ],
